    "alert_id": "ALERT_001",
    "title": "Rising creatinine and declining eGFR on TDF",
    "message": "Guidelines state that for patients on TDF, serum creatinine and eGFR should be monitored every 12 months, and if GFR is <60 mL/min, TDF should be substituted with ABC. This patient's creatinine has risen steadily from 0.9 (2020) to 1.4 mg/dL (2026), with eGFR now 52 mL/min/1.73m²—below the 60 mL/min threshold. TDF is a known cause of chronic kidney disease, acute kidney injury, and Fanconi syndrome. Evidence for drug attribution includes the progressive decline temporally linked to 3 years of TDF therapy and proteinuria on urinalysis. Evidence against includes the absence of Fanconi-type features (no bone pain, fractures, or marked polyuria) and stable weight without oedema. Occasional ibuprofen use (2–3 times weekly) may also contribute to renal impairment and compounds the risk.",
    "issue_type": "toxicity",
    "severity": "high",
    "recommended_action": "Substitute TDF with ABC (or AZT if ABC contraindicated) given eGFR <60. Do LFTs and RFTs. Counsel on avoiding NSAIDs including ibuprofen.",
    "citations": [
      {"page_number": 27, "chunk_id": "hiv_guidelines_art.extraction__hiv_guidelines_art.extraction__p27_c2"},
